L7:  * Resolution ladder (decision §4.6/§8 — degrade, never fail-closed):
L8:  *   1. OS keychain — macOS Keychain (`security`), Linux libsecret
L9:  *      (`secret-tool`), Windows DPAPI (ProtectedData, CurrentUser scope).
L10:  *   2. 0600 keyfile — ONLY when explicitly opted in; documented weaker
L11:  *      (a stolen directory/backup carries both secrets).
L12:  *   3. null — the caller degrades that process to memory-only operation;
L13:  *      conversations are never blocked.
L14:  *
L15:  * Threat-model boundary (documented in the decision and README): the
L16:  * keychain defends OFFLINE file theft (disk, backups, a stolen SGP.db); it
L17:  * does not defend malware already running inside the user's login session
L18:  * (macOS authorization prompts can be social-engineered; DPAPI/libsecret
L19:  * decrypt for the same user). This is the common OS-protection boundary,
L20:  * not unique to this design.
